A hands-off slow cooker dinner that yields fork-tender pork chops coated in a rich gravy made from condensed cream soups, beef broth, and fresh mushrooms.

*Adapted from [Spend With Pennies](https://www.spendwithpennies.com/crock-pot-pork-chops/#wprm-recipe-container-134606).*

## Ingredients

- 4 bone-in pork chops (about 3 pounds)
- Salt and black pepper, to taste
- ½ teaspoon paprika
- ½ teaspoon garlic powder
- 1 tablespoon olive oil
- 1 can (10.5 oz) condensed cream of mushroom soup
- 1 can (10.5 oz) condensed cream of chicken soup
- ¾ cup reduced-sodium beef broth
- 2 cups sliced mushrooms
- 1 small onion, sliced

## Instructions

1. **Sear the Chops:** Warm the olive oil in a wide skillet over medium-high heat. Rub both sides of the pork chops evenly with the paprika, garlic powder, salt, and black pepper. Lay the chops in the hot oil and sear for roughly 3 minutes per side to develop a flavorful golden-brown crust.
2. **Build the Pan Sauce:** Lift the chops out of the skillet and set them aside on a plate. Turn the burner heat down to medium, pour in the cream of mushroom soup, cream of chicken soup, and beef broth, and whisk vigorously across the skillet bottom to release and dissolve the caramelized drippings.
3. **Assemble in the Crock Pot:** Spread the sliced mushrooms and onions across the bottom of the slow cooker insert. Lay the seared pork chops directly on top of the vegetables, then ladle the warm soup mixture evenly over the chops.
4. **Slow Braise:** Place the lid on the slow cooker and cook on LOW for 7 to 8 hours until the pork yields effortlessly to a fork.
5. **Thicken and Plate:** If you prefer a heavier gravy, stir in a cornstarch slurry during the final 30 minutes on HIGH. Plate the chops over mashed potatoes, egg noodles, or white rice, and spoon the savory mushroom gravy generously over top.

## Notes

- **Thickening Slurry:** To thicken the pan juices into a dense gravy, blend 2 tablespoons cornstarch with 2 tablespoons cold water. Whisk into the slow cooker 20 to 30 minutes before serving and switch the setting to HIGH.
- **Pork Chops:** Bone-in pork chops hold up best to prolonged slow cooking without drying out. Thick-cut chops are recommended.
